* Tracing you can compile out.
*
* Remove the `@define TRACE` below and the marks are not skipped at run time --
* they are absent from the BrainFuck entirely. That is the difference between
* a conditional here and an `if` in the program: this one costs nothing when
* it is off, because there is nothing left of it.
*
* What it does, with `TRACE` defined and without:
*
*     def mark():
*         if TRACE:            # decided when the program is built, not run
*             say('.')
*
* A branch not taken is never expanded, so it may hold names and brackets that
* would be errors in a branch that is. The one at the bottom does.

@define TRACE 1

@var out

@macro say(letter) {
    @to out
    [-]
    +{letter}
    .
}

@macro mark {
    @ifdef TRACE
    @say('.')
    @endif
}

@say('H')
@mark
@say('i')
@mark
@say('!')

@ifdef NEVER
* Guarded on a name nothing defines, so this is skipped whichever way TRACE is
* set -- and it shows: `@to nowhere` names a cell that does not exist, and the
* bracket is unbalanced. Neither is an error, because neither is ever read as
* anything but text to step over.
@to nowhere
[
@endif
